const llmActionsProps = {"enabled":true,"providers":["claude","chatgpt","perplexity","gemini"],"placement":"header","copyButton":true,"footerLink":true,"markdownBody":"# Stage 08: Figures {#stage-08-figures}\n\nAdd two figures and an equation to *What is digital audio?*. Use a waveform image for the first figure and inline SVG for the second. Add an image map to *Recording your first track* with links from regions of a toolbar image.\n\nThe supplied images are generated illustrations of audio concepts and controls. Store them in `images/`, beside `topics/`. References from the topics therefore start with `../images/`.\n\n**Optional module.** See [Choose a learning path](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path) for the starting checkpoint and the next core lesson.\n\n**Time:** about 25 minutes. **You need:** stage 07 complete, and the two PNG files from the branch (see step 1).\n\nRecorded output below is an example. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. [Check your work](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/run-the-gate) to see the result for your own project.\n\n## Step 1: Add the images {#step-1-add-the-images}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Create `images/` and copy the two files** Take `images/waveform.png` and `images/device-toolbar.png` from the `tutorial/08-figures` branch, or draw your own; the image map coordinates in step 3 assume the branch's `device-toolbar.png`.\n\n   ```bash\n   mkdir images\n   git restore --source=origin/tutorial/08-figures -- images/waveform.png images/device-toolbar.png\n   ```\n2. **Write `images/README.md`** It records where the images came from and the MathML caveat below.\n\n   ```md {title=\"images/README.md\"}\n   Illustrations for the tutorial, generated (not screenshots): `waveform.png`\n   and `device-toolbar.png`. CC BY 4.0 like the rest of the project.\n\n   Note on equations: `what-is-digital-audio.dita` carries the decibel formula as\n   MathML inside `<equation-block>`, with a plain-text `<ph>` alternative beside\n   it. DITA-OT's html5 transform drops `<mathml>` (it is a `foreign` element)\n   unless a MathML plugin is installed, so the HTML shows the text form; a\n   processor that renders MathML shows the formula.\n   ```\n:::\n\n## Step 2: Figures, SVG and an equation {#step-2-figures-svg-and-an-equation}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Edit `topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita`** Two figures go into the *Waveforms* section, and the decibel formula goes into the *Amplitude* definition.\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n   +++ b/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n   @@ -13,6 +13,24 @@\n          <p>Sound travels through air as a continuous <term>waveform</term> of pressure changes.\n          A microphone converts these pressure changes into an electrical signal.\n          To store the signal digitally, the computer takes thousands of measurements, called <term>samples</term>, of the signal's amplitude every second.</p>\n   +      <fig id=\"fig-waveform\">\n   +        <title>A recorded waveform, with clipping at the right</title>\n   +        <image href=\"../images/waveform.png\" placement=\"break\" scale=\"80\">\n   +          <alt>A waveform whose peaks are cut flat at the right-hand end</alt>\n   +        </image>\n   +      </fig>\n   +      <p>Drawn schematically, one cycle of a pure tone looks like this:</p>\n   +      <fig>\n   +        <title>One cycle of a sine wave</title>\n   +        <svg-container>\n   +          <svg:svg xmlns:svg=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"320\" height=\"120\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 120\">\n   +            <svg:line x1=\"0\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n   +            <svg:path d=\"M0,60 C40,0 80,0 120,60 S200,120 240,60 S300,20 320,45\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3c78c8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n   +            <svg:text x=\"8\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">amplitude</svg:text>\n   +            <svg:text x=\"270\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">time</svg:text>\n   +          </svg:svg>\n   +        </svg-container>\n   +      </fig>\n        </section>\n        <section>\n          <title>Sample rate</title>\n   @@ -57,7 +75,22 @@\n            <dlentry>\n              <dt>Amplitude</dt>\n              <dd>The height of a sound wave, corresponding to how loud the sound is.\n   -          Measured in decibels (dB).</dd>\n   +          Measured in decibels (dB), a ratio against a reference level:\n   +          <equation-block>\n   +            <mathml>\n   +              <m:math xmlns:m=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\">\n   +                <m:mrow>\n   +                  <m:msub><m:mi>L</m:mi><m:mtext>dB</m:mtext></m:msub>\n   +                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>\n   +                  <m:mn>20</m:mn>\n   +                  <m:msub><m:mi>log</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:msub>\n   +                  <m:mfrac><m:mi>A</m:mi><m:msub><m:mi>A</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:msub></m:mfrac>\n   +                </m:mrow>\n   +              </m:math>\n   +            </mathml>\n   +            <ph>L (dB) = 20 log10 (A / A0)</ph>\n   +          </equation-block>\n   +          A level of <equation-inline><mathml><m:math xmlns:m=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mtext>dB</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:math></mathml><ph>−6 dB</ph></equation-inline> is half the amplitude of the reference.</dd>\n            </dlentry>\n            <dlentry>\n              <dt>Clipping</dt>\n   ```\n2. **Read the figure elements**\n\n   - `<fig>` is a figure: a `<title>` and some content, numbered in output. Give it an `@id` when something links to it, as `fig-waveform` is linked from the recording task.\n   - `<image href=\"…\">` places an image. `@placement=\"break\"` puts it on its own line rather than inline with the text; `@scale=\"80\"` renders it at 80 percent of its size. `@href` is relative to the topic file, hence `../images/`.\n   - `<alt>` is the text alternative, read by screen readers and shown when the image cannot be. Describe the information that the image conveys.\n   - `<svg-container>` holds inline SVG. The SVG elements must carry the `svg:` prefix, `<svg:svg xmlns:svg=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">`, because that is how the DITA DTDs declare them; an unprefixed `<svg>` does not validate.\n3. **Read the equation elements**\n\n   - `<equation-block>` is a displayed equation; `<equation-inline>` is one inside a sentence. Both are from the equation domain, which the base DTDs include.\n   - `<mathml>` holds MathML, here with the `m:` prefix bound to the MathML namespace. The formula is `L(dB) = 20 log10 (A / A0)`.\n   - The `<ph>` beside each `<mathml>` is a text alternative. The spec allows an equation to carry several representations; a processor uses the one it can render.\n:::\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> DITA-OT 4.3.5's HTML5 transform drops `<mathml>`, which is a `foreign` specialization, unless a MathML plugin is installed. That is why each equation keeps a `<ph>` text alternative beside the MathML: the HTML shows the text form, and a processor that renders MathML shows the formula. You can inspect this by rebuilding the guide after adding the figures.\n\n## Step 3: An image map {#step-3-an-image-map}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Edit `topics/recording-your-first-track.dita`** The Device Toolbar step gets an `<imagemap>`, and the waveform step gets a cross-reference to the figure by its id.\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n   +++ b/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n   @@ -22,13 +22,29 @@\n            <cmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the <uicontrol>Device Toolbar</uicontrol>.</cmd>\n            <info>\n              <p>The <uicontrol>Device Toolbar</uicontrol> is near the top of the window.\n   -          The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.</p>\n   +          The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.\n   +          Click a control in the picture to read about it.</p>\n   +          <imagemap>\n   +            <image href=\"../images/device-toolbar.png\">\n   +              <alt>The Device Toolbar: audio host, recording device, channels and playback device</alt>\n   +            </image>\n   +            <area>\n   +              <shape>rect</shape>\n   +              <coords>170,20,370,60</coords>\n   +              <xref href=\"preparing-to-record.dita\">Recording device</xref>\n   +            </area>\n   +            <area>\n   +              <shape>rect</shape>\n   +              <coords>380,20,490,60</coords>\n   +              <xref href=\"what-is-digital-audio.dita\">Channels</xref>\n   +            </area>\n   +          </imagemap>\n            </info>\n          </step>\n          <step>\n            <cmd>Click <uicontrol>Record</uicontrol>, or press <uicontrol>R</uicontrol>, to start recording.</cmd>\n            <info>\n   -          <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.</p>\n   +          <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone, like <xref href=\"what-is-digital-audio.dita#what-is-digital-audio/fig-waveform\"/>.</p>\n              <note type=\"warning\">A flat line instead of a waveform means the microphone is not selected correctly.\n              Click <uicontrol>Stop</uicontrol> and check the recording device.</note>\n            </info>\n   ```\n2. **Read the image map elements**\n\n   - `<imagemap>` is an `<image>` followed by one or more `<area>` elements. It is from the utilities domain.\n   - Each `<area>` has a `<shape>` (`rect`, `circle` or `poly`), `<coords>` in pixels of the image, and an `<xref>` for the link. Here the `<xref>` elements have content, so the link text is the given label rather than the target's title.\n   - The cross-reference `what-is-digital-audio.dita#what-is-digital-audio/fig-waveform` reaches the figure by its `@id`, the same `file#topic/element` form as a section link in stage 07.\n:::\n\n## Step 4: Update the README and check your work {#step-4-update-the-readme-and-check-your-work}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Change the \"You are on\" line and the layout** The layout section gains the `images/` folder.\n\n   ```diff {title=\"README.md\"}\n   --- a/README.md\n   +++ b/README.md\n   @@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n\n    Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n\n   -You are on stage 07: links.\n   +You are on stage 08: figures.\n\n    ## Stages\n\n   ```\n2. **Format and check your work** Format the topics:\n\n   ```bash\n   dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita\n   ```\n\n   Then choose **Project** > **Check Project** in the editor, or run `dogsbay-xml check .` from the project root. The output looks like this example:\n\n   ```\n   health   clean\n   build    full                 ok  /home/you/audacity-guide/out/full\n   output   clean (full)\n   Ready: the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and the output of full holds together.\n   ```\n:::\n\nThe health stage checks image references too. Misspell `waveform.png` in the `<fig>` as `wavefrom.png` and check your work. The check reports the broken reference with the file and line, and stops. The following output is an example:\n\n```\nhealth   NOT CLEAN\n  /home/you/audacity-guide/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita:18  @href=\"../images/wavefrom.png\" — target not found\n  (run project-health for the full report)\nNot ready: the project itself has faults. The build and the built output were not checked.\n```\n\nUndo the change and check again. Confirm that the check reports `Ready` before you continue.\n\n## Publish your changes {#publish-your-changes}\n\nAdd the lesson's topics to `audacity-guide.ditamap`, then check your work again. The check rebuilds the guide in `out/full/`. The complete map at this checkpoint is:\n\n```xml {title=\"audacity-guide.ditamap\"}\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Audio user guide</title>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/installing-audacity.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/removing-background-noise.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n</map>\n```\n\n```bash\ndogsbay-xml check .\n```\n\n## What you learned {#what-you-learned}\n\n- `<fig>` with `<title>` and an `@id`; `<image>` with `@href`, `@placement`, `@scale` and an `<alt>`.\n- Image paths are relative to the topic, so a sibling `images/` folder is `../images/`.\n- Inline SVG goes in `<svg-container>` with the `svg:` prefix.\n- `<equation-block>` and `<equation-inline>` carry `<mathml>` plus a `<ph>` text alternative; HTML5 output needs the alternative.\n- `<imagemap>`: an image, then `<area>` elements with `<shape>`, `<coords>` and an `<xref>`.\n- End of Part 1: nine topics, every base element you need for the body of a guide, published through the map and build introduced in stages 02 and 03.\n\n## Next lesson {#next-lesson}\n\nContinue with [Stage 09: map structure](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/part-2-maps/stage-09-map-structure).\n\nFor the core course, use the [learning path](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path) to skip optional modules.\n\n[Compare this stage with its predecessor](https://github.com/dogsbay/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial/compare/tutorial/07-links...tutorial/08-figures).\n","mdUrl":"https://dogsbay.ai/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/part-1-topics/stage-08-figures.md"} ?? undefined;
